[PERSPECTIVE B: PROSECUTION (Maximal Skepticism)]
The prosecution argues that the claim is CONTRADICTED or ungrounded because "World Chess Champion" in standard chess parlance specifically refers to the holder of the classical undisputed World Chess Championship (historically held by players like Magnus Carlsen, Garry Kasparov, etc., as referenced in Evidence 2 where "world champion Magnus Carlsen" is distinguished). Evidence 1 meticulously specifies that Nakamura is a "five-time U.S. Chess Champion, and the 2022 World Fischer Random Chess Champion." It never states that he became the general World Chess Champion. Equating a specialized variant title (Fischer Random) with the overarching title of World Chess Champion misrepresents his actual achievements and conflates distinct titles.

[PERSPECTIVE C: JUDICIAL ARBITRATION]
Evaluating the claim against the strict rules of evidence, the claim asserts that Hikaru Nakamura became the "World Chess Champion." In chess history and terminology, the "World Chess Championship" is a distinct, classical title traditionally held by players such as Magnus Carlsen (who is explicitly identified in Evidence 2 as the "world champion"). While Evidence 1 establishes that Nakamura is a "five-time U.S. Chess Champion" and the "2022 World Fischer Random Chess Champion," Fischer Random Chess is a distinct chess variant with its own specific world championship, separate from the primary World Chess Championship title. Conflating a variant championship with the title of World Chess Champion renders the broad claim false/contradicted by the specific framing of his actual titles provided in the text. 

